---
/*
 * The architecture diagram, kept to the rough structure: the laptop app, one fleet in the AWS
 * account (its foundation and a few agents), the tailnet joining laptop and agents, and the network
 * policy around them. One inline SVG whose colors all come from the tokens, its legend, and the
 * four numbered steps the diagram's orange markers refer to. The SVG keeps a fixed min-width and
 * scrolls inside .dg-wrap on narrow screens.
 */
import SectionHead from "./SectionHead.astro";
---
<section class="s" id="arch">
  <SectionHead num="§03" kicker="Architecture" title="No hosted service in the middle">
    {/* design.md §1 (Tailscale the only way in; outbound open), §2 System overview (core calls AWS directly, no server), §4.6/§4.8 and §5 (fleet_id on every resource; several fleets per account) */}
    <p>Your laptop and every agent sit inside your Tailscale tailnet, and that is the only way to reach an agent. Agents can reach the internet outbound; nothing on the internet can connect in. The app and CLI manage the fleet through AWS API calls straight to your account, with no hermetic server in between.</p>
  </SectionHead>
  {/* Diagram labels: design.md §2 (laptop/AWS topology), §3.1 (one core behind app and CLI), §4.8 (fleets share an account), §5 (foundation stack: agents/events tables, bucket, fleet IAM role, VPC), §5.1 (SSM secrets), §5.2 (tailnet policy), §6.3 (hermeticd installs Hermes, heartbeats), §7.2 (0 inbound rules, nftables, WireGuard), §1 (volumes outlive instances). */}
  <div class="dg-wrap">
<svg class="dg" viewBox="0 0 1200 660" role="img" aria-labelledby="dg-t dg-d">
<title id="dg-t">How hermetic fits together</title>
<desc id="dg-d">Your laptop runs the Hermetic app and CLI, which call your AWS account directly. In the account, one fleet has a foundation (agent records, a config bucket, an IAM role, secrets and a network) and a number of agents. Each agent is its own machine running Hermes and hermeticd, with its memory on a separate data volume. hermeticd reads its config from the foundation and reports health back. Your laptop and the agents share a Tailscale tailnet, which is the only way to reach an agent. The network policy has no inbound ports, so the internet cannot connect in, while agents can still reach out.</desc>
<defs>
<marker id="ah" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,0 L10,5 L0,10 z" style="fill:var(--fg)"/></marker>
<marker id="ahg" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,0 L10,5 L0,10 z" style="fill:var(--fg2)"/></marker>
<marker id="aho" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,0 L10,5 L0,10 z" style="fill:var(--ok)"/></marker>
</defs>

<!-- Tailnet: the laptop and the agents. The foundation sits outside it. -->
<path class="tnet" d="M8,118 H330 V290 H1124 V510 H330 V446 H8 Z"/>
